Thanks for the insight.  Here is the modified model where I ordered the means and use a normal prior instead of a uniform prior:

data {
  int<lower=1> K; // number of mixture components
  int<lower=1> N; // number of data points
  real y[N]; // observations
}
parameters {

  ordered[K] mu; // locations of mixture components

}
model {
  real ps[K]; // temp for log component densities

  mu ~ normal(0,50);

  for (n in 1:N) {
    for (k in 1:K) {
      ps[k] <- log(.25) + normal_log(y[n],mu[k], .25);
    }
    increment_log_prob(log_sum_exp(ps));
    }
}

But I'm still not converging and I'm getting these warnings:

Informational Message: The current Metropolis proposal is about to be rejected because of the following issue:
stan::prob::normal_log(N4stan5agrad3varE): Location parameter is inf:0, but must be finite!
If this warning occurs sporadically, such as for highly constrained variable types like covariance matrices, then the sampler is fine,
but if this warning occurs often then your model may be either severely ill-conditioned or misspecified.

Mark

