> On Jun 16, 2015, at 10:39 AM, Larry Hunsicker <lhuns...@mchsi.com> wrote:
>
> OK.  I think that I understand it.  (I used the "print()" trick within 'epil.stan' to clarify what was meant by y(n).)   In STAN, one can refer to a whole row of y with y[n] (unlike R, where one would have to write "y[n,]").  So the four values of y[n] are each matched up with the covariate[n] scalar values, and also with, in turn, each of the four values of V4_t, which can be referenced without an index.   Is this correct?

Correct.

> Just out of curiosity, within the STAN code is a length N*T vector of y and a corresponding (N*T)*# of covariates +1 (the extra one for V4_t) design matrix ever created?

No, we don't need to create the whole design matrix in general.

The only requirement for Stan is that the model define the log density.

- Bob

